from assessment_writer import write_review

def compact(sid, title, rows, appraisal):
    groups=[]
    for locator, cells, interpretation in rows:
        groups.append((locator, cells.split(' | '), interpretation))
    write_review(sid,title,groups,appraisal)

compact('P-ACTIV6-2026','ACTIV-6: a negative primary symptom endpoint and a secondary diagnosis signal',[
('Methods; protocol v12.0 Appendix G §21.7, printed pp108–109','Test early metformin against placebo for symptoms or death at day 180. | The long-term primary endpoint is specified in Appendix G, distinct from the platform acute primary endpoint. | Prevention after acute infection, not treatment of established long COVID.','The report must be interpreted by its own endpoint hierarchy.'),
('Methods; Table 1; participant flow','Adults at least 30 years old; median age 47, 63% women. | Ninety US sites; symptomatic infection within 7 days, positive test within 10 days; 83% reported prior vaccination or infection. | 3214 randomized; 231 excluded for administrative issues or medication not delivered within 7 days; 2983 analyzed, 1439 metformin and 1544 placebo.','The analysis is modified intention to treat, not all randomized participants.'),
('Methods: trial design and interventions','Aurobindo immediate-release metformin 500-mg tablets. | Oral 500 mg day 1, 500 mg twice daily days 2–5, then 500 mg morning and 1000 mg evening through day 14. | Usual clinical care; no simultaneous enrollment in other active platform drug comparisons. | Long-term surveys at days 90, 120 and 180.','This short acute regimen cannot establish the effects of chronic treatment.'),
('Methods: randomization and intervention','Placebo; 134 placebo recipients received generic placebo before the matching product became available. | Parallel 1:1 allocation. | Simple random-number sequence. | Double-blind design, with a reported early placebo-matching limitation. | Concurrent enrollment September 2023 to May 2024.','Differences in medication delivery and placebo appearance are relevant implementation limitations.'),
('Methods: outcomes; statistical analysis','Primary long-term endpoint was any COVID-attributed symptom or death at day 180; clinician diagnosis was secondary. | Self-report point-in-time symptom questionnaire and self-report of a clinician diagnosis since the preceding survey. | Primary day 180; additional day 90 and 120 assessments. | Patient-reported symptoms and diagnosis, not a laboratory surrogate. | NR: No established minimal important absolute risk reduction; the efficacy rule was posterior probability above 0.975.','A statistical decision threshold is not a patient-important change threshold.'),
('Abstract; Results; Table 2','Adjusted primary risks 2.9% metformin versus 3.6% placebo; raw symptoms 33 versus 46 at day 180. | Raw denominators 1439 and 1544 include missing observations; 1139 and 1251 supplied primary day-180 data. | Adjusted risk difference −0.8 percentage points, 95% credible interval −2.2 to 0.6; relative risk 0.79, 0.474 to 1.230. | Between-arm posterior probability of efficacy 0.83, below 0.975. Secondary clinician diagnosis adjusted risks 0.7% versus 1.4%; raw 8 versus 18. | Secondary absolute difference −0.7 points, credible interval −1.5 to 0.1; relative-risk interval reported 0.155–0.995, posterior probability 0.96.','Do not replace the negative primary decision with a positive claim based on a secondary relative-risk interval. Intervals computed on different scales need not have identical boundary behavior.'),
('Methods; Table 2; limitations','300 metformin and 293 placebo participants lacked day-180 primary data. | Bayesian Markov model used observed longitudinal data with adjustment; missing-at-random assumptions remain consequential. | NR: Full adherence distribution is not provided in this long-term report. | Administrative and late-delivery exclusions occurred after randomization; recent reinfection within 28 days was excluded for the long-term endpoint. | Modified intention-to-treat, 2983 medication recipients. | Individual participant, not each repeated survey.','About one fifth lacked the primary day-180 observation; modeling cannot prove their unobserved outcomes.'),
('Safety; Discussion','Safety of special interest, including lactic acidosis and self-reported hypoglycemia; general tolerability was not assessed here. | 1439 metformin and 1544 placebo in the modified intention-to-treat set. | No lactic acidosis reported; hypoglycemia reported by 2 metformin and 4 placebo recipients. | NR: This report does not give a comprehensive treatment-discontinuation analysis for intolerance. | Fourteen days of assigned treatment, long-term follow-up to 180 days.','These findings do not establish that gastrointestinal adverse effects were absent.'),
('Methods; Table 1; Table 2; Discussion; protocol Appendix G','Post-randomization exclusions, incomplete follow-up, self-report and low event frequency. | Randomization limits baseline confounding; selection after allocation and missing outcomes may reintroduce bias. | Protocol supports the long-term endpoint, but model regularization and efficacy threshold were updated after the day-28 analysis; registry appendix emphasizes acute outcomes. | Secondary analyses were not multiplicity-adjusted. | Primary and absolute secondary intervals include no effect; few diagnoses produce substantial uncertainty. | Day-90 placebo symptoms are 52 in Table 2 versus 51 in prose; hypertension is 20.9% in Table 1 versus 2% in prose. Protocol printed p109 describes futility at day180 then §21.7.3 says day90; both wordings retained.','Use table-specific numbers and retain discrepancies; neither one changes the primary null decision.'),
('Eligibility; Discussion','Largely previously immune US outpatients with acute infection. | Applies to this immediate-release 14-day schedule, not all products or chronic use. | The symptom primary endpoint differs from the clinician-diagnosis endpoint in COVID-OUT. | Preexisting long COVID was not excluded; fluctuating illness can be missed by point-in-time questions.','Do not describe this as a trial of treating established long COVID.'),
('Trial identifiers; references','ACTIV-6 metformin appendix, NCT06042855 under master NCT04885530. | Published acute-recovery report and earlier long-term preprint share participants with this report. | They are one randomized comparison, not independent replications. | Bounded comparison with COVID-OUT, Korean treatment trial and REVIVE; no exhaustive new search.','Count the published long-term paper once and supersede its preprint for these estimates.'),
('Byline; funding; conflicts','Carolyn Bramante, Thomas Stewart and ACTIV-6 investigators. | Multicenter US decentralized platform coordinated through Duke and collaborators. | NCATS support and BARDA contract; sponsor participated in design, conduct, analysis and publication. | Metformin product identified as Aurobindo; a product name does not itself establish donation. | Multiple investigators disclosed outside institutional grants, consulting and other industry relationships.','The report does not support a blanket claim of no conflicts or no sponsor role.'),
('Published PMC report; NCT04885530 and NCT06042855; protocol','Published Clinical Infectious Diseases 2026 report, DOI 10.1093/cid/ciag335, superseding the preprint for current estimates. | Full published article and current registry records retrieved. | GAP: Master protocol v12.0 retrieved and Appendix G inspected; its separately referenced SAP was not recovered. | Current appendix and master records inspected; this is not an audit of every historic registry revision. | No correction or retraction identified in the retrieved current records; internal discrepancies retained above. | Retrieved November 3–4, 2026.','Independent numerical verification is pending, separate from this source assessment.'),
('G-METFORMIN infection chapter','Early-infection prevention; comparison of symptoms versus clinician diagnosis. | Does not establish benefit for the primary symptom endpoint; provides a secondary diagnosis signal. | Observed results are distinguished from stronger preventive language used by authors; editorial conclusion remains uncertain.','Present both the primary failure and secondary signal together.'),
('Synthesis','Evidence for preventing a later clinician diagnosis is suggestive but the primary long-term symptom result was inconclusive. | Magnitude, durability and applicability to current infection patterns remain uncertain. | Replication with prespecified patient-important endpoints, complete follow-up and multiplicity control could strengthen or refute the signal.','This is evidence for discussion, not a personal prescribing instruction.')
], 'Well-sized blinded trial with low long-term event frequency and substantial missingness. Its primary symptom endpoint did not meet the prespecified decision rule. The secondary clinician-diagnosis signal must retain its absolute uncertainty, endpoint status and lack of multiplicity adjustment.')

compact('P-KOREA-2026','Korean long-COVID treatment trial: no improvement from a two-week course',[
('Abstract; Methods','Test metformin or UDCA for recovery from established PASC. | Metformin versus placebo was the powered primary comparison; UDCA was exploratory. | Recovery defined by crossing below a symptom-index threshold at week 8.','This is treatment, unlike acute-infection prevention trials.'),
('Methods; Table; Figure 1','Adults at least 19 with a previous positive test and PASC index at least 12; median age 36, 72% women, 97% vaccinated. | Two Seoul tertiary hospitals, infection 30–730 days earlier; upper limit expanded from 180 days during study. | 396 randomized, 132 per arm; abstract says 666 screened and Results says 658.','Preserve the screening discrepancy without changing the unambiguous randomized counts.'),
('Randomization and interventions','Oral metformin plus UDCA placebo. | Metformin titrated to 1500 mg/day for 14 days; exact formulation and daily steps deferred to inaccessible protocol. | Other arm received UDCA 900 mg/day plus metformin placebo; control received both placebos. | Visits weeks 1, 2, 4 and 8.','Do not infer the formulation from COVID-OUT or another trial.'),
('Methods','Double placebo. | 1:1:1 parallel allocation. | GAP: Full sequence generation and concealment details are in the separately linked protocol, unavailable here. | Participants, investigators and outcome assessors blinded. | Enrollment July 2024–January 2025; follow-up completed April 2025.','The report supports randomization but not an invented concealment mechanism.'),
('Outcomes','Primary PASC recovery at 8 weeks; continuous score, individual symptoms and cytokines secondary. | Weighted 12-domain index, range 0–34. | Week 8 primary, weeks 2 and 4 additional. | Patient-reported symptom index; cytokines are mechanistic surrogates. | Recovery threshold below 12 is a classification threshold, not established complete symptom resolution; design targeted 15 percentage points.','A participant can cross the threshold while remaining symptomatic.'),
('Primary and secondary outcomes; Figure 2','Recovery 84/132 metformin, 90/132 placebo and 90/132 UDCA. | Metformin 63.6%, placebo 68.2%. | Metformin-placebo difference −4.55 percentage points, 95% CI −15.98 to 6.88. | Score change −10.05 metformin versus −10.43 placebo; between-arm difference 0.38 points. | Score difference CI −1.42 to 2.18; within-arm improvement is not treatment efficacy.','The primary comparison shows no demonstrated benefit, with uncertainty spanning modest benefit and harm.'),
('Statistical analysis; Table footnote; supplement references','GAP: Detailed withdrawal flow and week-8 missing outcome counts require the linked supplement/figure data not fully acquired. | Sensitivity analyses for missing week-8 recovery and gray-zone scores described, but exact rules not independently reproduced. | GAP: Detailed adherence information deferred to protocol. | Two placebo participants lacked baseline PASC scores. | Intention-to-treat, all 396 randomized, for efficacy; at least one dose for safety. | Individual participant; repeated visits and cytokines are not additional people.','The common efficacy denominator should not be reused silently for the smaller safety set.'),
('Adverse events','Adverse events, serious events, hospitalizations and deaths assessed in those taking at least one dose. | GAP: Main report gives safety percentages but not all exact exposed denominators; do not infer denominators from rounding. | Any AE: 7 metformin (5.4%), 6 UDCA (4.6%), 3 placebo (2.3%); no metformin/UDCA serious events; one unrelated placebo arm fracture; no deaths. | GAP: Event-specific withdrawals are in unavailable Supplement Table 7. | Eight-week follow-up after 14-day treatment.','Mostly mild-to-moderate gastrointestinal events; sample and duration limit uncommon-harm detection.'),
('Methods; Results; Discussion','Blinding and randomization strengths; short treatment, selected younger volunteers, uncertain missing-data detail and threshold-based endpoint limitations. | Baseline similarity limits measured confounding; selection limits external validity. | GAP: Full protocol/SAP comparison remains unavailable despite public main-text access. | Secondary cytokine and symptom comparisons unadjusted for multiplicity. | Placebo recovery 68.2% greatly exceeded assumed 15%; primary CI excludes the targeted 15-point benefit but not smaller benefit. | Abstract screening 666 versus Results 658; Discussion describes 70–80% recovery while main ITT recovery is 66.7%.','Record these differences rather than silently harmonizing them.'),
('Eligibility; Discussion','Younger mostly vaccinated adults with generally mild PASC. | Two-week oral metformin course only. | Week-8 threshold recovery does not establish complete functional recovery or long-term trajectory. | Limited applicability to severe, persistent or differently defined long COVID.','A null trial of this regimen does not prove all possible regimens ineffective.'),
('Registration; current inventory','KCT0009342, independent Korean trial. | COVID-OUT and ACTIV-6 are prevention comparators; REVIVE a distinct treatment trial. | UDCA and metformin share placebo; do not count shared participants twice. | Current guide-source scope only.','This contributes one metformin treatment comparison.'),
('Byline; funding statement','So Yun Lim, Jacob Lee, Sung-Han Kim and colleagues. | Asan Medical Center and Hallym University Kangnam Sacred Heart Hospital, Seoul. | Korean National Institute of Infectious Diseases/National Institute of Health; stated no role in design, analysis or publication. | NR: Supplied-product funding details not established in the accessible report. | GAP: Linked individual disclosure forms were not acquired.','Do not convert missing forms into a no-conflict assertion.'),
('Publisher article and linked materials','Published Annals of Internal Medicine 2026, DOI 10.7326/ANNALS-25-04883. | Full publisher text read and preserved as rendered DOM and visible text. | GAP: Supplement, protocol/SAP and data-sharing links located but not successfully retrieved. | KCT0009342 identified in report; separate registry record not inspected. | No correction/retraction identified in current article/metadata; screening discrepancy retained. | Publisher accessed November 3; assessment November 4, 2026.','Main-report assessment is substantive but the full packet remains source-limited.'),
('G-METFORMIN infection chapter','Established long-COVID treatment. | Contradicts a claim that this two-week course improves week-8 recovery. | Authors conclude no significant improvement; observed estimates support that bounded statement.','Do not substitute preventive trial results for this treatment result.'),
('Synthesis','No demonstrated clinical advantage over placebo for this regimen and population. | Longer treatment, severe phenotypes and durable function remain unanswered. | Better phenotyping, complete outcome reporting and longer, independently replicated trials could change the conclusion.','Do not recommend UDCA or metformin based on improvements shared with placebo.')
], 'The randomized primary comparison was null. Substantial spontaneous or nonspecific improvement makes within-arm change misleading. Inaccessible supplement/protocol details are explicitly preserved as gaps and constrain the assessment, without preventing an accurate account of the reported primary result.')
